  },
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "inter-graph-edge-semantics-declaration",
      "type": "declaration",
      "label": "Inter-Graph Edge Semantics",
      "position": {
        "x": -520,
        "y": -440
      },
      "width": 400,
      "height": 250,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "inter-graph-edge-semantics",
          "nodeId": "inter-graph-edge-semantics",
          "name": "Inter-Graph Edge Semantics",
          "version": "0.2.0",
          "description": "A recursive Q&A contract for graph-local edges, Bridge-projected handles, and ordinary workspace-owned edges between rendered graph-nodes."
        },
        "intent": {
          "kind": "investigation",
          "scope": "local"
        },
        "document": {
          "url": "github://twilite-zone/public/tasks/inter-graph-edge-semantics/root.node"
        },
        "declaration": {
          "kind": "investigation",
          "targetMode": "artifact",
          "defaultSurfaceId": "",
          "surfaces": []
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "problem",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "What feels off?",
      "position": {
        "x": 0,
        "y": -440
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 330,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#241d32",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# What feels off?\n\nWe are treating several workspace lines as if they were interchangeable:\n\n- a Portal following a Port\n- an export/import Bridge pair\n- an authored edge crossing a graph-document seam\n- an edge between two rendered semantic nodes\n- a derived workspace route\n\nThey may look similar, but they do not necessarily have the same identity or authority."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-visible-line",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q1 · Is every workspace line an edge?",
      "position": {
        "x": -900,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nIs every line drawn between node regions a graph edge?\n\n## Answer\n\nNo. A Portal binding visualizes traversal or shared presentation. A relationship drawn between projected Bridge handles is a regular edge owned by the containing workspace graph. Geometry alone does not decide which one it is."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-bridge",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q2 · Is a Bridge an edge?",
      "position": {
        "x": -440,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nDoes a Bridge own an edge, or does it expose/consume a place where an edge may connect?\n\n## Answer\n\nA Bridge is a graph-local boundary node. At the containing workspace level it projects as a typed handle on the rendered graph-node. The workspace edge connects projected handles; the Bridge does not own that outer edge."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-owner",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q3 · Who owns edge type?",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhere does semantic edge-class identity belong?\n\n## Answer\n\nOn the edge at the graph level that owns it. An inner graph owns its local edge to a Bridge. The workspace graph owns its edge between rendered graph-nodes. A Bridge constrains the classes accepted by its projected handle."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-one-or-two",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q4 · One edge or two?",
      "position": {
        "x": 480,
        "y": 20
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhen a relationship appears continuous across graph levels, is it one edge or several?\n\n## Answer\n\nIt is a recursive composition of ordinary edges. Each edge has one owning graph and never crosses that graph's boundary. Inner edges terminate on Bridge nodes; an outer workspace edge connects the Bridges' projected handles."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-same-node",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q5 · Same node, different graphs",
      "position": {
        "x": -900,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nIf two graph documents declare the same semantic `nodeId`, what changes?\n\n## Answer\n\nThey render inside one semantic-node boundary, but each document remains an ownership seam. Authored edges remain local to their document. Any containing-level relationship still belongs to the containing graph, even when the visual node boundary is shared."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-different-node",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q6 · Different semantic nodes",
      "position": {
        "x": -440,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nWhat permits an edge between different rendered semantic nodes?\n\n## Answer\n\nThe containing workspace graph authors a regular edge between compatible projected Bridge handles. Opening graphs, following a Portal, or matching adjacency does not itself create that edge."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-portal",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q7 · Do Portals carry edge classes?",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nDo Portals carry edge classes?\n\n## Answer\n\nNo. A Portal identifies and follows a destination Port. Its workspace line is traversal presentation. If a separate gesture authors a relationship, that creates an ordinary edge with its own class at the accepting graph level."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "q-bridge-class",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Q8 · How does a Bridge constrain type?",
      "position": {
        "x": 480,
        "y": 360
      },
      "width": 420,
      "height": 260,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#172033",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Question\n\nHow does a Bridge participate in edge typing?\n\n## Answer\n\nA Bridge exposes or consumes a typed relationship capability. Its projected handle declares allowed or required edge classes. Compatibility governs whether the containing graph may create an edge; the created edge owns its selected class."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "candidate",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Candidate synthesis — not yet law",
      "position": {
        "x": -620,
        "y": 720
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#18302b",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Revised model — implementation target\n\n1. **Every edge is regular at the graph level that owns it.**\n2. **An authored edge never crosses its owning graph/document boundary.**\n3. **An inner edge terminates on a graph-local Bridge node.**\n4. **At the next zoom level, a rendered graph is a node and its Bridge is a projected handle.**\n5. **The containing workspace is a graph and owns ordinary edges between those handles.**\n6. **Each owning level gives its edge an ID, class, label, route, and lifecycle.**\n7. **Bridge compatibility constrains edge creation; it does not transfer edge ownership to child documents.**\n8. **Portal bindings remain navigational and visually distinct from edges.**\n9. **Same `nodeId` combines graph renderings inside one node boundary without erasing document ownership seams.**"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "counterexample",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Counterexample hunt",
      "position": {
        "x": 80,
        "y": 720
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#38231d",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Questions still open\n\n- Is the current workspace session itself a persistable graph document, or an ephemeral graph awaiting materialization?\n- How does an outer edge retain endpoints when one child graph closes?\n- Can one projected Bridge handle accept multiple outer edges?\n- May inner and outer edges use different classes?\n- How are edge-class constraints declared: allowed set, required class, or typed capability?\n- What happens when a restored workspace cannot load one endpoint graph?\n- When should a workspace graph be promoted to a durable authored graph?"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "plan",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Investigation plan",
      "position": {
        "x": -620,
        "y": 1200
      },
      "width": 650,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#1d2940",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Implementation plan\n\n1. Give workspace state its own ordinary `edges` collection.\n2. Store outer endpoints by workspace graph-node instance and projected Bridge handle.\n3. Stop writing newly created outer edges into child declarations.\n4. Persist workspace edges with workspace arrangement state; keep legacy `edgeReferences` readable during migration.\n5. Render workspace edges through the normal edge-style contract, including edge classes.\n6. Make Bridge projections advertise class compatibility to edge creation.\n7. Draw Portal bindings with a separate traversal presentation.\n8. Add fixtures for local-edge-to-Bridge, typed outer edge, closed endpoint, incompatible class, and same-node multi-document rendering."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "acceptance",
      "type": "markdown",
      "label": "Decision gate",
      "position": {
        "x": 80,
        "y": 1200
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 430,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "style": {
        "background": "#2d2440",
        "color": "#eef4ff",
        "borderColor": "#52627d",
        "borderWidth": 1,
        "borderStyle": "solid",
        "borderRadius": 16
      },
      "data": {
        "markdown": "# Acceptance contract\n\n- No newly authored edge names a node outside its owning graph.\n- Creating an outer edge dirties workspace state, not either child graph.\n- The outer edge survives layout changes and child focus changes.\n- Its selected edge class drives workspace rendering.\n- Bridge handles reject incompatible edge classes.\n- Closing and reopening a child graph preserves the workspace edge identity.\n- Portal lines cannot be mistaken for authored relationship edges.\n- The same edge abstraction works for node-to-node edges inside a graph and graph-node-to-graph-node edges inside a workspace."
      }
    }
  ],
